# THE 20-MINUTE CONSTRAINT — read this before anything else

A full eval run takes ~20 minutes on your local model. That single fact reshapes the plan, because naive k=5 repeats means **100 minutes per run and 200 minutes for an A/B pair.** You would spend the weekend watching a progress bar.

## Split generation from scoring

This is the highest-leverage change in the whole document.

**The 20 minutes is *generation*, not scoring.** Right now they are fused, so every time you tweak a metric you pay full inference cost again. Separate them:

```
eval generate --config <cfg> --k 3     # slow: ~20 min x k. Writes raw outputs to disk.
eval score <generation_id>             # fast: re-scores cached outputs. Seconds to minutes.
```

What this buys you:

- **Metric development becomes free.** Write a new scorer, run it over yesterday's cached outputs, see results in seconds. Without this, every metric iteration costs 20 minutes — and you will simply stop iterating.
- **Calibration becomes free** — Phase 3 replays scorers over stored outputs with zero inference.
- **Generation moves to wall-clock time, not your time.** Kick off a k=5 run at dinner; it is done after dinner.
- **Reproducibility improves** — the exact text that was scored is on disk permanently. This is also what makes your calibration labels stable.

Store generations as `evals/generations/<gen_id>/` alongside the config that produced them, and have run records reference `generation_id`. A "run" becomes generation + scoring pass, and the two version independently.

## Two-tier metrics

Your metric picks make this fall out naturally — **two of the three are deterministic**:

| Tier | Metrics | Cost | Run it |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Fast** | Hallucinated entity rate, knowledge-boundary violations | Graph queries over cached text. **Seconds.** | Every save, if you like |
| **Slow** | Keyword + tone LLM judge | LLM inference | On demand / overnight |

The fast tier needs **no LLM at all** — cached output text checked against Neo4j. That is your tight iteration loop, and it sidesteps the 20-minute problem entirely.

## Revised k guidance

| Purpose | k | Generation cost | When |
|---|---|---|---|
| Building scorers | reuse cached outputs | 0 | Continuously |
| Iterating on config | k=3 on a 5-8 case subset | ~5 min | During the day |
| Real comparison | k=3 full set | ~60 min | Over lunch / while building |
| A number you would quote | k=15 full set | ~5 hours | **Start Sunday evening, read Monday** |

I earlier said k=5 minimum. With 20-minute runs, **k=3 is the honest working floor and k=15 is an overnight job**, not an afternoon one. Do not quote a k=3 number as if it were tight — the confidence interval will show you why.

