# What this becomes on your resume

Replacing the current *"Validated engine via a two-phase LLM-judge reaching 90+% precision"* — a number with no method behind it — with something like:

> Built an evaluation pipeline for the dialogue engine: a versioned golden set scored by deterministic graph checks (hallucinated-entity rate, knowledge-boundary violations against the Neo4j state) alongside a two-phase LLM judge, with each scorer calibrated against a hand-labelled set and reported as a confusion matrix. Separated generation from scoring so cached outputs could be re-scored without re-inference, ran each case k times to turn binary verdicts into per-case pass rates with confidence intervals, and A/B tested graph retrieval configurations and prompt versions to catch regressions before release.

The generation/scoring split is worth keeping in the bullet — it's an engineering decision driven by a real constraint (20-minute inference), and interviewers respond to constraint-driven design far better than to feature lists.

Why this is a different class of claim:

- **It leads with judge calibration**, which is the rarest thing you have. "I validated my evaluator" is a sentence most senior engineers can't say about their own systems.
- **It names methods, not adjectives** — golden set, confusion matrix, confidence intervals, A/B comparison.
- **It admits the instrument is imperfect**, which reads as calibration rather than weakness. Overclaiming is the more common failure and it's easy to catch in an interview.
- **It ends on a decision the measurement enabled** — catching regressions — rather than on a number.

It also maps almost word-for-word onto the stated requirements: Anthropic's "evaluation frameworks", Glean's "offline and online techniques, designing experiments", Harvey's "evals & harnesses that capture real-world quality", and Cohere's "move beyond trial and error."

**Interview question this now answers.** *"How do you know your eval measures anything real?"* — most candidates have nothing. You have a labelled set, a reproducible command, and a matrix.
